                        "idea_summary": "Intelligent garments that can diagnose their own cleanliness by detecting microbial load or specific stains at a molecular level. Upon detection, the fabric can either signal the user about the precise location needing a spot treatment or automatically trigger a localized release of an embedded cleaning agent.",
                        "core_principle": "Miniaturized biosensors integrated into textile fibers can detect specific biomarkers of soiling (e.g., microbial metabolites, urea) and trigger a co-localized cleaning or signaling response.",
                        "form_factor": "Smart textiles with embedded biosensor and micro-actuator networks.",
                        "mode_of_action": "1. Biosensors woven into the fabric continuously monitor for soiling biomarkers. 2. When a contaminant threshold is passed, the sensor triggers an action. 3. This could be a visual signal (e.g., a color-changing thread), a notification to a smart device, or the activation of co-located microcapsules containing enzymes. 4. Cleaning becomes highly efficient, data-driven, and resource-optimal.",
