                        "optimized_idea": "Competitive Exclusion Biofilms for Proactive Hygiene Management",
                        "idea_summary": "Instead of killing all microbes, this approach fosters a healthy, protective microbiome on the surface of the clothing. This 'good' biofilm acts as a living barrier, preventing harmful or odor-causing bacteria from gaining a foothold, thereby maintaining hygiene through ecological balance rather than sterilization.",
                        "core_principle": "A harmless, stable biofilm of beneficial microbes is cultivated on the fabric surface to outcompete and inhibit the growth of undesirable, odor-causing, or pathogenic bacteria.",
                        "form_factor": "A fabric finish or 'primer' that encourages the colonization of specific beneficial microbes while deterring others.",
                        "mode_of_action": "1. A textile is treated with nutrients or pre-colonized with a consortium of beneficial, non-pathogenic microbes. 2. This established biofilm occupies the available surface area and resources on the fabric. 3. When exposed to harmful or odor-causing bacteria, the protective biofilm outcompetes them for space and nutrients, preventing their proliferation. 4. Hygiene is maintained by managing the microbial ecosystem on the garment.",
