                        "optimized_idea": "Mechanobactericidal Nanotopography Textiles",
                        "idea_summary": "Inspired by the wings of cicadas and dragonflies, this concept creates clothing with a bactericidal surface that kills microbes physically, not chemically. This non-leaching, 'always-on' sanitation method maintains hygiene without the use of chemicals or the risk of promoting microbial resistance.",
                        "core_principle": "Engineers the fabric surface with a permanent, microscopic physical topography (e.g., nanopillars) that mechanically ruptures the cell walls of bacteria upon contact.",
                        "form_factor": "A synthetic fiber with a bio-inspired texture extruded directly into its surface, or a permanent finish applied via nanoimprinting.",
                        "mode_of_action": "1. The fabric surface is covered in microscopic, high-aspect-ratio pillars or needles. 2. When a bacterium lands on the surface, its cell membrane drapes over the nanopillars. 3. The physical stress induced by the topography stretches and eventually tears the membrane, killing the bacterium. 4. The process is continuous and requires no energy, water, or chemical input.",
