                        "optimized_idea": "Self-Cleaning Textiles with Integrated Abiotic Catalysts",
                        "idea_summary": "Fabrics incorporating non-biological catalysts or enzyme-mimicking nanoparticles directly into the fibers. These embedded catalysts actively break down organic soils and pollutants upon contact or with minimal activation (e.g., ambient humidity, sonic waves), creating a continuously self-cleaning surface.",
                        "core_principle": "Employs material-integrated catalysis, where synthetic molecules or nanoparticles mimic enzymatic action to chemically degrade unwanted substances without being consumed in the process.",
                        "form_factor": "Yarn or finished textile with catalytic nanoparticles embedded within or coated onto the fibers.",
                        "mode_of_action": "1. Organic soil (e.g., oils, sweat components) comes into contact with the catalytic sites on the fabric. 2. The catalyst facilitates a chemical reaction (e.g., oxidation) that breaks the soil down into smaller, harmless, and volatile molecules. 3. The cleaning action is continuous and passive, or can be enhanced with an external stimulus like ultrasound.",
