                    "uuid": "ec658f92-7f5e-4ddd-b68a-1fa70d35b1d2",
                    "group_name": "Bio-Catalytic Brine Cleaning",
                    "group_summary": "This theme focuses on using biological catalysts, specifically enzymes from extremophiles, that operate effectively in high-salinity, low-water environments. The strategic relevance lies in creating highly potent cleaning systems that are decoupled from fresh water resources, making them ideal for arid regions or closed-loop systems.",
                    "descriptive_title": "Extremophile Enzyme Systems for Water-Independent Fabric Decontamination",
                    "one_liner_summary": "This approach utilizes enzymes from salt-loving microorganisms to power a cleaning process within a highly saline, low-water medium.",
                    "selling_point": "By using catalysts that are inherently stable and active in brine, this method enables effective fabric cleaning with minimal to no reliance on fresh water.",
                    "curated_ideas": [
                      {
                        "optimized_idea": "Extremophile Enzyme Brine for Ultra-Low Water Garment Cleaning",
                        "idea_summary": "This concept proposes a cleaning solution using enzymes derived from halophilic archaea, which thrive in high-salt environments. This allows for effective cleaning and sanitation of clothing in a concentrated brine, drastically reducing the need for fresh water.",
                        "core_principle": "Leverages enzymes from extremophilic organisms (halophiles) that maintain high catalytic activity in low water activity, high-solute concentration environments.",
                        "form_factor": "A concentrated liquid or gel containing stabilized halophilic enzymes, intended to be mixed with a small amount of saline water to create a cleaning brine.",
                        "mode_of_action": "1. The concentrated enzyme solution is added to a small volume of brine (salt water). 2. Garments are soaked or agitated in this brine. 3. The halophilic enzymes break down organic stains and microbial contaminants, which are sequestered in the saline solution. 4. The brine can potentially be filtered and reused, while clothes are dried with minimal rinsing.",
                        "source_sparks": [
                          {
                            "idea": "Halophilic archaea enzymes for potent, low-water 'brine' cleaning of clothing."
                          }
                        ]
                      }
                    ],
