                        "idea_summary": "A two-stage system that first uses specific, targeted enzymes to break down soils in a low-water wash, then purifies this 'greywater' using a compact MBR. This approach optimizes both cleaning efficacy and water recyclability by separating the soil-degrading agent (enzymes) from the water purification mechanism.",
                        "core_principle": "Combines the specificity of enzymatic catalysis for soil removal with the high purification efficiency of a membrane bioreactor for water recycling.",
                        "form_factor": "A washing appliance connected to an external or integrated compact MBR unit for water purification and reuse.",
                        "mode_of_action": "1. Clothes are washed with a small amount of water and a specific enzyme cocktail. 2. The enzyme-rich water breaks down soils. 3. The used water is pumped to an MBR unit. 4. The MBR filters out particulates and allows microbes to consume dissolved organics, producing clean water to be reused in the next cycle.",
