                        "idea_summary": "A textile designed to host a dormant, engineered microbial consortium that becomes metabolically active when exposed to body heat and moisture. The microbes consume sweat, oils, and odor-causing compounds, effectively 'self-cleaning' the garment during wear or with a simple misting.",
                        "core_principle": "Bio-integration of thermophilic and oleaginous microorganisms into fabric structures, which activate metabolically in response to combined heat and moisture cues.",
                        "form_factor": "A woven or non-woven fabric with engineered porosity and surface chemistry designed to support a dormant, symbiotic biofilm.",
                        "mode_of_action": "1. Dormant, engineered microbes reside within the fabric's interstitial spaces. 2. Body heat (>30°C) and moisture (>60% RH from sweat or mist) co-activate the microbes. 3. The activated microbes metabolize organic soil and volatile organic compounds. 4. The garment is continuously refreshed, drastically reducing the need for machine washing.",
