                        "idea_summary": "A fabric with a built-in, powerful antimicrobial function that kills microbes on contact. Unlike single-use treatments, this antimicrobial capability can be fully 'recharged' through a simple, minimal-water process, providing long-lasting, reliable hygiene protection.",
                        "core_principle": "N-halamine compounds are chemically bonded to textile fibers (e.g., cellulose). These compounds possess a biocidal chlorine atom that can be repeatedly replenished by exposure to a chlorine source like bleach.",
                        "form_factor": "A textile treated with N-halamine precursors, making the antimicrobial property an intrinsic and regenerable feature of the fabric.",
                        "mode_of_action": "1. The N-halamine-treated fabric neutralizes microbes on contact during wear. 2. Over time and with exposure to organic matter, the active chlorine is consumed. 3. To recharge, the garment is briefly exposed to a dilute chlorine solution (e.g., a light mist or quick dip). 4. The N-halamine structure captures chlorine from the solution, fully restoring its antimicrobial potency.",
