                        "idea_summary": "This concept transforms clothing into a diagnostic tool that reports its own hygiene status in real-time, moving from a schedule-based to a needs-based cleaning paradigm. It provides users with objective data on when and where a garment actually needs to be cleaned, eliminating unnecessary washing.",
                        "core_principle": "Embedded chemiresistive, optical (SERS), or biological sensors within the textile continuously monitor the presence of specific microbial metabolites, volatile organic compounds (VOCs), or pathogens.",
                        "form_factor": "Apparel with invisible, integrated sensor threads or patches that can communicate wirelessly with a smartphone app or a smart home system.",
                        "mode_of_action": "1. As a garment is worn, integrated sensors detect rising levels of target analytes (e.g., VOCs from sweat, specific bacterial proteins). 2. When a pre-set hygiene threshold is crossed, the sensor sends a signal to the user's phone. 3. The app can indicate the level and location of soiling, recommending either a spot treatment or a full wash. 4. Post-cleaning, the sensor confirms that hygiene levels have been restored.",
